WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:07.470
<v Speaker 1>Imagine the smartest person in the entire world has this incredibly profound secret to tell you.

2
00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:08.870
<v Speaker 2>A life-altering truth, yeah.

3
00:00:08.870 --> 00:00:09.310
<v Speaker 1>Exactly.

4
00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:13.670
<v Speaker 1>But before they speak a single word to you, they make you walk away from the crowds.

5
00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:14.670
<v Speaker 2>Leave it all behind.

6
00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.910
<v Speaker 1>You walk completely out of the bustling city.

7
00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.990
<v Speaker 1>You go past the very last houses, out into a graveyard.

8
00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:21.950
<v Speaker 2>Just absolute silence.

9
00:00:21.950 --> 00:00:23.110
<v Speaker 1>There's the silent tombs.

10
00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:26.590
<v Speaker 1>And they wait until the only things surrounding you are the dead.

11
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:31.230
<v Speaker 1>Only then do they deliver their gravest, most important counsel.

12
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.550
<v Speaker 2>Sets a mood, doesn't it?

13
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:33.310
<v Speaker 1>Really does.

14
00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:35.230
<v Speaker 1>Welcome to this deep dive.

15
00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:39.230
<v Speaker 1>Today we are opening Reza Shakazemi's spiritual ethos.

16
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:43.390
<v Speaker 1>And we are exploring this really intense frontier.

17
00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:44.710
<v Speaker 2>The boundary line, essentially.

18
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:51.910
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, the exact boundary where human thinking just, well, it exhausts its standard tools and something entirely different has to take over.

19
00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.630
<v Speaker 2>It's a difficult transition to talk about.

20
00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:54.270
<v Speaker 1>It is.

21
00:00:54.430 --> 00:01:06.270
<v Speaker 1>We are looking at why the most exalted speech about God is built almost entirely out of denials and at the same time, why the human intellect must ultimately be lived out in a perpetual war against one's own soul.

22
00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:09.190
<v Speaker 2>Which sounds completely contradictory at first glance.

23
00:01:09.310 --> 00:01:09.870
<v Speaker 1>Totally.

24
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:16.870
<v Speaker 1>But to guide us, we have this single organizing reality that will anchor our whole conversation today.

25
00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.870
<v Speaker 1>It goes like this.

26
00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:25.630
<v Speaker 1>The intellect is a living spiritual faculty realized only through the war to win the whole soul over to its side.

27
00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.550
<v Speaker 2>It is such a bracing place to start.

28
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:28.990
<v Speaker 2>Oh, definitely.

29
00:01:29.230 --> 00:01:32.670
<v Speaker 2>Because it throws us immediately into a profound crisis of perception.

30
00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:40.190
<v Speaker 2>To understand what actually happens at that frontier of the mind, we really have to sit with those two distinct puzzles you just mentioned.

31
00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.910
<v Speaker 1>The speech and the graveyard.

32
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:42.350
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

33
00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:44.350
<v Speaker 2>The first puzzle is structural.

34
00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:52.190
<v Speaker 2>How is it that the most precise soaring speech ever uttered about the divine operates by stripping away our words?

35
00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.150
<v Speaker 1>Just taking them apart.

36
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:53.630
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

37
00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.710
<v Speaker 2>It is this patient, unyielding litany of what God is not.

38
00:01:56.710 --> 00:01:59.150
<v Speaker 2>And then the second puzzle is the setting itself.

39
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:04.670
<v Speaker 2>Why does the wisest man of his age require the mute company of the graveyard before delivering his teaching?

40
00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.430
<v Speaker 1>Like why go to those extremes?

41
00:02:06.590 --> 00:02:06.830
<v Speaker 2>Right.

42
00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:15.710
<v Speaker 2>I mean we really have to feel the sheer stakes of these mysteries before we attempt any answers at all because they are both asking the exact same fundamental question.

43
00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.070
<v Speaker 1>Which is what?

44
00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:25.950
<v Speaker 2>Does it truly take to break through the reasoning surface of the mind and actually access the deeper perception that the intellect is built for?

45
00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:31.950
<v Speaker 1>So to explore this, we're looking really closely at the language and the life of Ali, peace be upon him.

46
00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.830
<v Speaker 1>When we look at how he speaks of the divine, there is this honest, almost

47
00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:42.910
<v Speaker 1>I don't know, uncomfortable tension between the spiritual depths of the intellect and its rational surface.

48
00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.510
<v Speaker 2>Uncomfortable is a good word for it.

49
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:47.230
<v Speaker 1>And the book insists we have to start at that rational surface.

50
00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.750
<v Speaker 1>We can't just bypass it.

51
00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:49.790
<v Speaker 2>Oh, absolutely not.

52
00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:51.550
<v Speaker 2>The mind has to begin with concepts.

53
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.710
<v Speaker 2>We use words like mercy, power, nearness, reality.

54
00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:56.430
<v Speaker 1>Basics.

55
00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:57.390
<v Speaker 2>The basics, yes.

56
00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.910
<v Speaker 2>The intellect really has no other equipment to set out on the journey.

57
00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:03.070
<v Speaker 1>So you can't just throw out logic on day one.

58
00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:06.950
<v Speaker 2>No, to reject concepts or despise the rational

59
00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:11.790
<v Speaker 2>logical surface of the mind as somehow unspiritual.

60
00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:13.790
<v Speaker 2>That is a massive mistake.

61
00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:21.470
<v Speaker 2>The revelation given by the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, provides the mind with these exact concepts.

62
00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:22.990
<v Speaker 1>They give us a place to stand.

63
00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.430
<v Speaker 2>They're the initial footholds.

64
00:03:24.830 --> 00:03:26.910
<v Speaker 2>But then we hit the discontinuity.

65
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.110
<v Speaker 2>We hit the absolute boundary of what language can actually do.

66
00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:35.310
<v Speaker 1>And this brings us to this foundational concept in the text regarding how human language functions.

67
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:37.790
<v Speaker 1>We need to look at the idea of definition.

68
00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:38.350
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

69
00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.550
<v Speaker 2>The Arabic understanding of definition is highly instructive here because it carries a dual mechanism.

70
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:44.990
<v Speaker 1>Meaning it does two things at once.

71
00:03:44.990 --> 00:03:45.710
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

72
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:50.990
<v Speaker 2>A definition is the act of fixing what a concept is, but crucially it also means a limit.

73
00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.390
<v Speaker 1>Like a physical edge.

74
00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:53.790
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

75
00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:57.110
<v Speaker 2>To define a thing is to literally draw an edge around it.

76
00:03:57.110 --> 00:03:58.990
<v Speaker 2>This is our first governing analogy here.

77
00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:01.070
<v Speaker 2>Definition as a drawn edge.

78
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.830
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I can picture that.

79
00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:06.030
<v Speaker 2>Bounding is what makes a concept functional in our daily lives.

80
00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:13.590
<v Speaker 2>I mean, if you define a physical object, you draw a conceptual boundary around it so you can distinguish it from something else.

81
00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:16.270
<v Speaker 1>So drawing the edge separates this from that.

82
00:04:16.270 --> 00:04:19.390
<v Speaker 1>It creates a useful limitation so we can actually talk about things.

83
00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:20.350
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

84
00:04:21.230 --> 00:04:27.950
<v Speaker 2>But consider the mechanical failure that occurs when the reality you are seeking is, by its very nature, limitless.

85
00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.470
<v Speaker 1>Because it doesn't have an edge.

86
00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:30.270
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

87
00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:39.790
<v Speaker 2>When you attempt to define the limitless, every single act of definition, no matter how careful or reverent you are of being, misses the mark by its own success.

88
00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:45.150
<v Speaker 1>Oh, because the very act of drawing the edge creates a boundary that the limitless reality instantly outruns.

89
00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:45.710
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

90
00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:49.070
<v Speaker 2>The concept fixes a shape, but God has no shape.

91
00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:55.550
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, going beyond the limits set by rational definition requires a faculty that operates beyond reason.

92
00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:56.470
<v Speaker 1>Not against it.

93
00:04:56.470 --> 00:04:57.870
<v Speaker 2>Beyond its edges, yes.

94
00:04:57.870 --> 00:04:59.150
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I have to stop you there.

95
00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:03.390
<v Speaker 1>I don't buy that yet if every concept of God is negated, what is left to worship?

96
00:05:03.550 --> 00:05:05.390
<v Speaker 2>That reaction is completely natural.

97
00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:11.710
<v Speaker 2>It is exactly the panic the rationalist mind feels when its primary tools are taken away.

98
00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:13.870
<v Speaker 1>It feels like jumping into a void.

99
00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:14.510
<v Speaker 2>It does.

100
00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:22.510
<v Speaker 2>But the defense against that panic is understanding the sharp difference between a strictly rationalistic view and a truly spiritual view.

101
00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:23.470
<v Speaker 1>How do they differ?

102
00:05:23.470 --> 00:05:26.910
<v Speaker 2>Well, rationalism treats reason as the entirety of existence.

103
00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:30.830
<v Speaker 2>If a reality eludes clear, bounded concepts,

104
00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.470
<v Speaker 2>The rationalist dismisses it as unreal, or like you said, as a void.

105
00:05:35.470 --> 00:05:36.950
<v Speaker 1>Because they can't draw an edge around it.

106
00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:37.710
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

107
00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:44.750
<v Speaker 2>The spiritual view, however, keeps the net of reason gladly, but it knows the largest realities will always slip through the mesh.

108
00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:50.070
<v Speaker 1>So the danger is mistaking our own mental boundaries for the boundaries of reality itself.

109
00:05:50.350 --> 00:05:51.270
<v Speaker 2>You hit the nail on the head.

110
00:05:51.550 --> 00:06:00.710
<v Speaker 2>If you equate your personal logical reasoning with the totality of the intellect, you shrink the vast objective reality of revelation down to the private specific

111
00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:02.030
<v Speaker 2>of your own brain.

112
00:06:02.110 --> 00:06:04.030
<v Speaker 1>You make God small enough to fit in your head.

113
00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:04.750
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

114
00:06:05.630 --> 00:06:08.110
<v Speaker 2>Reason initiates the movement toward truth.

115
00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:09.950
<v Speaker 2>It simply cannot complete it.

116
00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:16.190
<v Speaker 2>The surface of the intellect is real and it is useful, but the ground beneath it goes down infinitely further.

117
00:06:16.190 --> 00:06:17.390
<v Speaker 1>And what's down there?

118
00:06:17.390 --> 00:06:21.470
<v Speaker 1>Like what faculty takes over when reason hits its drawn edge.

119
00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:22.190
<v Speaker 2>The heart.

120
00:06:22.430 --> 00:06:25.070
<v Speaker 1>Okay, and the text makes a really critical distinction here.

121
00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.830
<v Speaker 1>We are not talking about the heart as the seat of emotion or sentiment.

122
00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.430
<v Speaker 1>No, not at all.

123
00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:32.990
<v Speaker 1>use the word today for feelings.

124
00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:37.790
<v Speaker 1>We are talking about an organ of profound perception.

125
00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:41.470
<v Speaker 2>We must distinguish carefully between outward sight and inner vision.

126
00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.870
<v Speaker 2>Outward sight is the ordinary mechanical faculty of the physical eyes.

127
00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:50.430
<v Speaker 2>Inner vision is a spiritual perception lit by the verities of faith.

128
00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:51.550
<v Speaker 1>Verities of faith.

129
00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:53.150
<v Speaker 1>What does that mean in this context?

130
00:06:53.150 --> 00:07:01.550
<v Speaker 2>These are deep structural realities disclosed to someone who believes, opening a capacity in the heart to perceive what no physical eye could ever process.

131
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:04.190
<v Speaker 1>Ali was actually asked a direct question about this, wasn't he?

132
00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:04.750
<v Speaker 2>Was.

133
00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:12.750
<v Speaker 1>A man asked him if he had ever seen his Lord, and his answer just forces us to completely reevaluate what seeing actually means.

134
00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:19.870
<v Speaker 1>He said that eyes do not see him through the observation proper to sight, but hearts see him through the verities of faith.

135
00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.350
<v Speaker 2>The precision of his answer is so vital.

136
00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:30.030
<v Speaker 2>He decisively rules out the physical eyes, because God is not an object in the physical universe possessing edges that light can strike.

137
00:07:30.430 --> 00:07:33.230
<v Speaker 1>Right, I mean, yeah, you can't bounce photons off the divine.

138
00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:33.950
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

139
00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:36.670
<v Speaker 2>But notice he does not retreat into mere poetry.

140
00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:39.710
<v Speaker 2>He affirms a genuine literal seeing.

141
00:07:40.270 --> 00:07:43.550
<v Speaker 2>He simply relocates the mechanism to its proper faculty.

142
00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:44.350
<v Speaker 1>The heart.

143
00:07:44.430 --> 00:07:44.990
<v Speaker 2>The heart.

144
00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:51.710
<v Speaker 2>And scripture backs this up entirely, asserting that it is not the eyes that grow blind, but the hearts within the breasts that grow blind.

145
00:07:51.910 --> 00:07:53.230
<v Speaker 1>That is a terrifying thought.

146
00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:57.550
<v Speaker 2>It is, because if you have perfectly functioning physical eyes, but your inner eye is dark,

147
00:07:57.910 --> 00:08:02.910
<v Speaker 2>So we arrive back at that threshold.

148
00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:04.510
<v Speaker 1>Reason has drawn its edge.

149
00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:05.630
<v Speaker 1>It can go no further.

150
00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:08.110
<v Speaker 1>The heart has to take over the work of perception.

151
00:08:08.350 --> 00:08:15.470
<v Speaker 1>But how does a mind, which has been heavily trained to rely only on bounded concepts its whole life, actually cross that boundary?

152
00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:20.590
<v Speaker 1>Like how do we transition from thinking about a truth to perceiving it directly?

153
00:08:21.230 --> 00:08:22.830
<v Speaker 2>It is carried across by paradox.

154
00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:27.230
<v Speaker 2>In this spiritual ethos, paradox is not a rhetorical flourish.

155
00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.030
<v Speaker 2>It is a vital working mechanism.

156
00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:30.670
<v Speaker 1>How does it work?

157
00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:40.270
<v Speaker 2>Because there is an unbridgeable gap between reality as it is in itself and the tiny human concepts we form of it, that gap has to be felt by the seeker.

158
00:08:40.270 --> 00:08:42.270
<v Speaker 2>It cannot just be stated as a dry fact.

159
00:08:42.270 --> 00:08:43.710
<v Speaker 1>So paradox is the tool.

160
00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:47.630
<v Speaker 2>Paradox is the instrument designed to make you feel the failure of the concept.

161
00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:49.470
<v Speaker 1>This is apophatic discourse then.

162
00:08:49.630 --> 00:08:51.550
<v Speaker 1>Speech that precedes by unsaying.

163
00:08:51.550 --> 00:08:57.590
<v Speaker 2>Yes, is the deliberate architecture of affirming an attribute and immediately denying the boundary of that attribute.

164
00:08:57.670 --> 00:08:58.030
<v Speaker 2>attribute.

165
00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.630
<v Speaker 2>You have to take the human conception of reality apart, piece by piece.

166
00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:02.790
<v Speaker 1>It sounds destructive.

167
00:09:02.830 --> 00:09:04.110
<v Speaker 2>It's an unbuilding.

168
00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.950
<v Speaker 2>A negation clears the ground.

169
00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:10.590
<v Speaker 2>It creates a vast empty space where a deeper inner vision can finally rise.

170
00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:13.510
<v Speaker 1>We see the absolute summit of this with the Prophet.

171
00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:13.950
<v Speaker 2>We do.

172
00:09:14.190 --> 00:09:22.430
<v Speaker 1>In the physical posture of prostration, placing the highest part of the human head onto the lowest dust of the earth, he reached a height of spiritual nearness.

173
00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:27.550
<v Speaker 1>where he admitted his total inability to capture the divine in words.

174
00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:30.270
<v Speaker 2>He said, I cannot enumerate your praise.

175
00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.430
<v Speaker 2>You are as you have praised yourself.

176
00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:34.910
<v Speaker 1>The admission of failure is the highest praise.

177
00:09:34.990 --> 00:09:37.710
<v Speaker 2>Because the reality forever outruns the word sent after it.

178
00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:42.510
<v Speaker 1>Ali takes this foundation and builds an incredible structure of paradox upon it.

179
00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.110
<v Speaker 1>We find this in his collected sermons, the peak of eloquence.

180
00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:50.670
<v Speaker 2>The opening of the very first sermon in that collection executes this perfectly.

181
00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.270
<v Speaker 2>It begins by offering praise,

182
00:09:52.750 --> 00:09:55.870
<v Speaker 2>does so by immediately confessing that all praise falls short.

183
00:09:55.870 --> 00:10:00.110
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, that no speech, no human reckoning can possibly deliver what is actually owed.

184
00:10:00.230 --> 00:10:02.550
<v Speaker 2>And then it introduces this organizing reality.

185
00:10:02.550 --> 00:10:07.070
<v Speaker 2>It says the divine attribute has no binding limitation, no existing description.

186
00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:09.190
<v Speaker 1>It is such a difficult phrase to sit with.

187
00:10:09.790 --> 00:10:14.190
<v Speaker 1>The attributes say mercy or power is affirmed as absolutely real.

188
00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:18.910
<v Speaker 1>Yet in the exact same breath, it is declared to have no existing description.

189
00:10:19.150 --> 00:10:21.950
<v Speaker 2>It's pulling the rug out from under your conceptual mind.

190
00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:28.750
<v Speaker 1>And then the sermon constructs this ladder of realization, says the foremost thing in religion is the knowledge of God.

191
00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:29.470
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

192
00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:31.790
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of that knowledge is belief.

193
00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:34.830
<v Speaker 1>The perfection of belief is affirming oneness.

194
00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:37.550
<v Speaker 1>And the perfection of oneness is sincerity.

195
00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:40.830
<v Speaker 2>And then comes the shock that dismantles the mind's comfort.

196
00:10:41.710 --> 00:10:46.110
<v Speaker 2>The perfection of that sincerity is to divest God of the attributes.

197
00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:50.630
<v Speaker 1>You climb this ladder, affirming on one rung, only to negate on the next.

198
00:10:50.630 --> 00:10:58.030
<v Speaker 2>The text gives you the words to approach the divine and then ruthlessly strips those very words away so you cannot turn them into idols.

199
00:10:58.270 --> 00:11:03.150
<v Speaker 1>Okay, but that sounds like wordplay isn't affirming and denying 1 attribute a contradiction?

200
00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:08.670
<v Speaker 2>It looks like a contradiction to the reasoning mind, sure, but it is actually a highly engineered jolt.

201
00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:09.390
<v Speaker 1>A jolt.

202
00:11:09.390 --> 00:11:09.710
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

203
00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:14.030
<v Speaker 2>To understand the mechanics of it, look at the pattern of the first testimony of Islam.

204
00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:18.030
<v Speaker 2>it pairs a stark negation with an absolute affirmation.

205
00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:23.870
<v Speaker 2>First, the denial, there is no God, then the exception, except God.

206
00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:26.350
<v Speaker 1>So the negation removes the false limit.

207
00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:27.150
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

208
00:11:27.430 --> 00:11:31.790
<v Speaker 2>What is being destroyed is not the attribute of mercy or justice itself.

209
00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.870
<v Speaker 2>but the human limitation that clings to our conception of it.

210
00:11:35.990 --> 00:11:36.990
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I think I see.

211
00:11:37.030 --> 00:11:40.430
<v Speaker 1>When we conceptualize mercy, we make it a bounded thing.

212
00:11:40.590 --> 00:11:41.950
<v Speaker 1>We draw an edge around it.

213
00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:42.510
<v Speaker 2>We do.

214
00:11:42.510 --> 00:11:44.030
<v Speaker 2>We make it separate from justice.

215
00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.790
<v Speaker 2>We make it separate from the essence of God.

216
00:11:45.950 --> 00:11:49.310
<v Speaker 1>But the attribute in its reality is entirely one with the essence.

217
00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:49.710
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

218
00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:55.310
<v Speaker 2>So the spiritual meaning of mercy is kept, but the boundary the mind drew around it dissolves.

219
00:11:55.950 --> 00:12:03.590
<v Speaker 2>The shock of the contradiction proves to the seeker that their mental portrait of the absolute is flat, forcing them to look for the depth.

220
00:12:03.630 --> 00:12:05.030
<v Speaker 1>It forces the heart to open.

221
00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.190
<v Speaker 2>A later poet captured this emptying beautifully.

222
00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.670
<v Speaker 2>He described it as a ladder of ascent.

223
00:12:10.910 --> 00:12:12.670
<v Speaker 2>Dying is mineral to become plant.

224
00:12:12.830 --> 00:12:14.430
<v Speaker 2>Dying is plant to rise to animal.

225
00:12:14.430 --> 00:12:15.950
<v Speaker 2>Dying is animal to become human.

226
00:12:16.030 --> 00:12:19.670
<v Speaker 1>So every time you let go of a bounded smaller self, you move upward.

227
00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:21.950
<v Speaker 2>The emptying leaves you with the fullness of reality.

228
00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:29.230
<v Speaker 1>If the intellect has to transcend the reasoning faculty in this dramatic way, we have to look at where the intellect comes from in the 1st place.

229
00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:36.590
<v Speaker 1>The sermon collection shifts from these dizzying heights of paradox down to the very mud of human creation.

230
00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:38.750
<v Speaker 2>This is our second governing analogy.

231
00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.750
<v Speaker 2>The spirit breathed into clay.

232
00:12:40.750 --> 00:12:43.950
<v Speaker 1>It's a very grounded image after all that abstract theology.

233
00:12:43.950 --> 00:12:44.510
<v Speaker 2>It is.

234
00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:49.470
<v Speaker 2>The sermon unfolds the creation of the human being in a slow, deliberate scene.

235
00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:54.270
<v Speaker 2>The creator gathers earth hard and soft, sweet and salty.

236
00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:55.790
<v Speaker 2>Water is worked into it.

237
00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.150
<v Speaker 1>Kneaded and shaped.

238
00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.790
<v Speaker 2>Kneaded and shaped into a form with joints and limbs.

239
00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.590
<v Speaker 2>It is hardened until it rings like sounding clay.

240
00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:10.190
<v Speaker 1>If you picture that perfectly finished biological form, it has eyes, it has ears, the physical organ of the brain is entirely set within the skull.

241
00:13:10.430 --> 00:13:12.110
<v Speaker 1>Nothing is structurally missing.

242
00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:13.950
<v Speaker 1>And yet it's just sounding clay.

243
00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.430
<v Speaker 1>It can neither see nor hear nor think.

244
00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:19.950
<v Speaker 2>It is only when the spirit is breathed into it that the form stands up

245
00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:25.350
<v Speaker 2>as a living human being endowed with the power to reflect and with knowledge to tell true from false.

246
00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:28.750
<v Speaker 1>So the biological organs did not generate the life or the consciousness.

247
00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:29.390
<v Speaker 2>No.

248
00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.110
<v Speaker 2>The life was breathed into the organs.

249
00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.670
<v Speaker 2>It is literally an inspiration.

250
00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:40.910
<v Speaker 2>The single breath produces both life and intellect in the exact same instant.

251
00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:45.910
<v Speaker 1>So the capacity to think is not an accessory bolted onto an animal frame later on.

252
00:13:45.910 --> 00:13:46.350
<v Speaker 2>Never.

253
00:13:46.590 --> 00:13:49.870
<v Speaker 1>The bestowal of the spirit is the bestowal of consciousness.

254
00:13:50.030 --> 00:13:56.350
<v Speaker 1>This is why treating the intellect as just a data processing machine is a total failure to understand what it is.

255
00:13:56.430 --> 00:14:00.270
<v Speaker 2>To be true to the intellect is exactly the same thing as being spiritual.

256
00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:04.870
<v Speaker 1>And that profound unity defines what the tradition calls the true.

257
00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:07.630
<v Speaker 2>Which is very different from our modern definition.

258
00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:13.150
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, today we think an intellectual is someone who retains a lot of information or wins debates on TV.

259
00:14:13.150 --> 00:14:16.510
<v Speaker 2>But the true intellectual is the one who puts each thing in its proper place.

260
00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.910
<v Speaker 1>Which is the exact definition of justice?

261
00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

262
00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:28.350
<v Speaker 2>Only the true intellectual can be fully just because only the one who perceives reality without the distortion of their own ego can set things where they truly belong.

263
00:14:28.590 --> 00:14:34.590
<v Speaker 1>Right thought, right action, and the realization of the real are one single unified faculty.

264
00:14:34.670 --> 00:14:36.030
<v Speaker 2>They cannot be separated.

265
00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:44.910
<v Speaker 1>But if that is the standard for the true intellectual, we have to face the terrifying question of what it actually costs to become such a person.

266
00:14:44.990 --> 00:14:46.030
<v Speaker 2>The price is deep.

267
00:14:46.390 --> 00:14:48.590
<v Speaker 1>What does it demand of a human life?

268
00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.350
<v Speaker 1>And this is where we reach the pivot of the entire teaching.

269
00:14:52.910 --> 00:14:55.550
<v Speaker 1>We have to follow Ali on a walk into the graveyard.

270
00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:57.390
<v Speaker 2>The setting is entirely deliberate.

271
00:14:57.790 --> 00:15:03.070
<v Speaker 2>The commander of the faithful takes his student, the disciple Kumail ibn Ziyad, by the hand.

272
00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.190
<v Speaker 1>Just the two of them.

273
00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:05.150
<v Speaker 2>They leave Kufa.

274
00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:09.550
<v Speaker 2>They walk until the markets, the noise, and the living city are entirely left behind.

275
00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:12.190
<v Speaker 2>They walk out among the mute company of the dead.

276
00:15:12.190 --> 00:15:12.750
<v Speaker 1>Why there?

277
00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.510
<v Speaker 2>You wait for this isolation because

278
00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:19.630
<v Speaker 2>The teaching he's about to deliver dismantles the entire value system of the city they just left.

279
00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:24.990
<v Speaker 1>Surrounded by tombs, he tells his disciple that hearts are vessels and the best of them hold the most.

280
00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.990
<v Speaker 1>And then he ruthlessly sorts all of humanity into 3 categories.

281
00:15:29.070 --> 00:15:30.910
<v Speaker 2>The sorting is uncompromising.

282
00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:34.350
<v Speaker 1>He says there is the lordly knower anchored securely in the Lord.

283
00:15:34.430 --> 00:15:37.310
<v Speaker 1>There's the seeker pursuing knowledge for deliverance.

284
00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:39.150
<v Speaker 1>And then there is the common crowd.

285
00:15:39.150 --> 00:15:40.270
<v Speaker 2>The drifting crowd.

286
00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:49.590
<v Speaker 1>The text likens this third group to moths hurling themselves blindly into any flickering flame, drifting with every passing current, seeking no firm support.

287
00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:54.190
<v Speaker 2>To save his disciple from becoming a moth drifting on the winds of circumstance.

288
00:15:54.590 --> 00:16:00.830
<v Speaker 2>He draws this incredible contrast between knowledge and wealth across 5 specific dimensions.

289
00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:04.510
<v Speaker 1>He dissects exactly why the city's priorities are backwards.

290
00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:05.470
<v Speaker 2>Let's look at them.

291
00:16:05.950 --> 00:16:08.030
<v Speaker 2>First, he points out the burden of security.

292
00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:13.470
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge stands guard over you, whereas you must spend your life energy standing guard over your wealth.

293
00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:17.630
<v Speaker 1>The mental bandwidth required just to protect assets is immense.

294
00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:19.630
<v Speaker 1>Wealth requires a fortress.

295
00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.150
<v Speaker 2>It makes you a prisoner of your own money.

296
00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.830
<v Speaker 1>But inner knowledge is a fortress.

297
00:16:23.070 --> 00:16:25.710
<v Speaker 1>It secures your character for making destructive choices.

298
00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:26.350
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

299
00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:29.310
<v Speaker 2>The second dimension is the law of increase.

300
00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.550
<v Speaker 2>Wealth decreases every time you spend it.

301
00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.310
<v Speaker 2>is a zero-sum game.

302
00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:35.390
<v Speaker 1>You hand over a coin, you don't have the coin anymore.

303
00:16:35.470 --> 00:16:39.070
<v Speaker 2>But knowledge actively grows the more freely it is dispersed.

304
00:16:39.550 --> 00:16:46.910
<v Speaker 2>When you empty your mind to teach another person, you are forced to clarify your own understanding, which deepens your grasp of the truth.

305
00:16:47.150 --> 00:16:49.150
<v Speaker 1>The third dimension deals with longevity.

306
00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.430
<v Speaker 1>The fruits of wealth vanish the moment the money is gone.

307
00:16:52.510 --> 00:16:54.990
<v Speaker 2>The status, the comforts, the fair-weather allies.

308
00:16:55.310 --> 00:16:56.590
<v Speaker 2>They evaporate instantly.

309
00:16:56.670 --> 00:17:02.110
<v Speaker 1>But no turn of fortune, no economic collapse can strip a human being of their inner knowledge.

310
00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.270
<v Speaker 1>It remains permanently intact.

311
00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.870
<v Speaker 2>The 4th dimension fundamentally changes how we view intelligence.

312
00:17:08.430 --> 00:17:13.150
<v Speaker 2>He says the acquisition of knowledge is a religion, a debt repaid to the maker.

313
00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:13.710
<v Speaker 1>A debt.

314
00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:17.630
<v Speaker 2>The intellect is not your private property to use for your own amusement.

315
00:17:18.110 --> 00:17:25.150
<v Speaker 2>It is a profound gift breathed into your clay, and the use of it to seek truth is the repayment of a cosmic debt.

316
00:17:25.470 --> 00:17:27.310
<v Speaker 1>And the 5th dimension is about command.

317
00:17:27.630 --> 00:17:30.110
<v Speaker 1>Knowledge is a judge that actively directs your life.

318
00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:34.430
<v Speaker 1>Wealth is just a passive thing that is continually judged, managed, and disposed of.

319
00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.030
<v Speaker 2>Knowledge is the active force.

320
00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.270
<v Speaker 2>Wealth is merely a material tool.

321
00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:46.990
<v Speaker 1>He concludes this comparison by stating that those who hoard wealth perish while they are still breathing, but true knowers endure as long as time endures.

322
00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:53.710
<v Speaker 1>But even as he delivers the soaring praise of knowledge, he presses his hand to his chest and mourns.

323
00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:55.950
<v Speaker 2>The grief is palpable in the text.

324
00:17:56.030 --> 00:18:00.190
<v Speaker 1>He mourns the absolute lack of worthy vessels to carry this truth.

325
00:18:00.670 --> 00:18:03.150
<v Speaker 1>He lists the broken vessels he sees around him.

326
00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:04.030
<v Speaker 2>Who does he see?

327
00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:09.230
<v Speaker 1>The student who is sharp but untrustworthy, who weaponizes religion for personal gain.

328
00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:15.310
<v Speaker 1>The blindly obedient follower whose faith shatters at the first sign of doubt because they have no inner insight.

329
00:18:15.710 --> 00:18:20.030
<v Speaker 1>The person greedy for pleasure, led by their appetites, the hoarder.

330
00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:24.510
<v Speaker 2>He is entirely surrounded by people, yet profoundly isolated.

331
00:18:24.670 --> 00:18:26.910
<v Speaker 1>But the council shifts into a prayer.

332
00:18:27.070 --> 00:18:32.110
<v Speaker 1>He declares that the earth will never be left empty of a hidden few who uphold the proof of God.

333
00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:35.150
<v Speaker 2>They might be the smallest in number, but they hold the greatest rank.

334
00:18:35.310 --> 00:18:39.230
<v Speaker 1>The present imam of the time echoes this, reminding us that knowledge is a trust.

335
00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:41.710
<v Speaker 1>debt discharged solely by giving it away.

336
00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.550
<v Speaker 2>These hidden few are not just librarians preserving historical facts.

337
00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:48.990
<v Speaker 2>They are the living channels through which reality anchors the earth.

338
00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:51.870
<v Speaker 1>And here we reach the most critical sentence of the Graveyard Council.

339
00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:55.790
<v Speaker 1>Ali says that through them, knowledge penetrates the reality of insight.

340
00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:56.910
<v Speaker 2>The verb is key.

341
00:18:56.990 --> 00:19:01.630
<v Speaker 1>Yes, the verb used for penetrates brings us to our third governing analogy.

342
00:19:02.110 --> 00:19:04.350
<v Speaker 1>The army storming the commander.

343
00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:07.630
<v Speaker 2>The word implies a violent strategic assault.

344
00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:08.430
<v Speaker 1>How so?

345
00:19:08.830 --> 00:19:10.030
<v Speaker 2>Think of ancient warfare.

346
00:19:10.310 --> 00:19:13.630
<v Speaker 2>An army is structured with a right wing, a left wing, and a center.

347
00:19:13.870 --> 00:19:16.270
<v Speaker 2>At the absolute center stands the commander.

348
00:19:16.270 --> 00:19:17.310
<v Speaker 1>Giving the orders.

349
00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:26.350
<v Speaker 2>The objective of an assault is to smash through the flanks, ignore the distractions on the wings, and drive a wedge straight into the heart of the enemy line to capture the commander.

350
00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.710
<v Speaker 1>And once you take the commander.

351
00:19:27.710 --> 00:19:29.550
<v Speaker 2>You possess the entire field.

352
00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:30.750
<v Speaker 2>The battle is won.

353
00:19:30.750 --> 00:19:33.550
<v Speaker 1>So knowledge is not just data sitting on a page.

354
00:19:33.710 --> 00:19:34.430
<v Speaker 1>It contains

355
00:19:34.590 --> 00:19:36.430
<v Speaker 1>a latent explosive force.

356
00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:42.190
<v Speaker 1>The student has to wage an effortful assault to storm past the outer wall of concepts.

357
00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:52.150
<v Speaker 2>And when that knowledge finally breaks through and seizes the heart, it takes the command of the limbs, the thoughts, and the actions, exactly as a victorious general takes command of a conquered kingdom.

358
00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.510
<v Speaker 1>The interiorization is total.

359
00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.870
<v Speaker 1>There is no seam left between what they know and what they are.

360
00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:02.270
<v Speaker 2>This requires an immense exertion, which brings us directly to our central spine.

361
00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:02.910
<v Speaker 1>Let's hear it.

362
00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:10.590
<v Speaker 2>The intellect is a living spiritual faculty realized only through the war to win the whole soul over to its side.

363
00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:14.830
<v Speaker 1>The realization of the intellect demands the absolute orientation of the soul.

364
00:20:15.150 --> 00:20:19.150
<v Speaker 1>And you cannot orient the soul toward reality if it is clogged with vice.

365
00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:19.950
<v Speaker 2>No, you cannot.

366
00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:22.750
<v Speaker 1>In this vision, vice is not just bad manners.

367
00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:24.590
<v Speaker 1>It is a metaphysical obstruction.

368
00:20:24.910 --> 00:20:28.350
<v Speaker 1>It is a rust that spreads across the perceptive surface of the heart.

369
00:20:28.350 --> 00:20:31.470
<v Speaker 2>If the heart is the organ capable of seeing the verities of faith,

370
00:20:32.030 --> 00:20:34.510
<v Speaker 2>then vice actively blinds that inner eye.

371
00:20:34.750 --> 00:20:38.510
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the true intellectual is in a state of perpetual war.

372
00:20:39.310 --> 00:20:42.910
<v Speaker 1>Uprooting ignorance and dissolving vice are the exact same act.

373
00:20:42.990 --> 00:20:47.470
<v Speaker 2>You cannot see reality clearly if your ego is constantly distorting the lens.

374
00:20:47.470 --> 00:20:48.990
<v Speaker 1>Wait, this seems grim.

375
00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:52.350
<v Speaker 1>If the struggle is against the soul, isn't the soul the enemy?

376
00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:54.350
<v Speaker 1>Are we talking about destroying our own selves?

377
00:20:54.350 --> 00:20:57.150
<v Speaker 2>That is the most dangerous misunderstanding of this entire tradition.

378
00:20:57.150 --> 00:20:57.510
<v Speaker 1>How so?

379
00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.310
<v Speaker 2>It is not a war against the soul.

380
00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:01.150
<v Speaker 2>It is a war for the soul.

381
00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:01.910
<v Speaker 2>To understand

382
00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:02.510
<v Speaker 2>The terrain.

383
00:21:03.070 --> 00:21:05.310
<v Speaker 2>Picture 3 figures standing on a battlefield.

384
00:21:06.190 --> 00:21:09.710
<v Speaker 2>On one side, you have the intellect leading the forces of the compassionate.

385
00:21:10.190 --> 00:21:16.350
<v Speaker 2>On the opposing side, you have caprice, human whim, ego, appetite, commanding the forces of the devil.

386
00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:17.950
<v Speaker 1>And the soul sits in the middle.

387
00:21:17.950 --> 00:21:18.590
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

388
00:21:19.150 --> 00:21:20.350
<v Speaker 2>The soul is the prize.

389
00:21:20.590 --> 00:21:23.950
<v Speaker 2>It is being pulled by caprice on one side and the intellect on the other.

390
00:21:24.510 --> 00:21:26.910
<v Speaker 2>The goal of the intellectual is not to destroy the soul.

391
00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:30.110
<v Speaker 2>The goal is to break the tyrannical grip of caprice

392
00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:33.790
<v Speaker 2>liberate the soul, and enlist it entirely on the side of the intellect.

393
00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:35.190
<v Speaker 1>Okay, I see the difference.

394
00:21:35.190 --> 00:21:37.630
<v Speaker 1>It's a rescue mission, not a search and destroy.

395
00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:39.790
<v Speaker 1>You're winning the soul over, not annihilating it.

396
00:21:39.790 --> 00:21:40.510
<v Speaker 2>Precisely.

397
00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:43.470
<v Speaker 2>You are rescuing the soul from hidden idolatry.

398
00:21:44.270 --> 00:21:47.470
<v Speaker 2>Caprice is the ego setting itself up as a false god.

399
00:21:47.630 --> 00:21:48.190
<v Speaker 1>Sneaky.

400
00:21:48.190 --> 00:21:48.670
<v Speaker 2>Very.

401
00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:56.350
<v Speaker 2>The text describes this hidden idolatry as being more concealed than a black ant crawling across a dark stone on a moonless night.

402
00:21:56.430 --> 00:21:57.630
<v Speaker 1>Almost impossible to see.

403
00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:04.590
<v Speaker 2>Because the ego is setting itself up as the center of the universe, you cannot defeat the ego using the tools of the ego.

404
00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:07.790
<v Speaker 2>You need the higher force of the intellect to break its hold.

405
00:22:08.030 --> 00:22:12.110
<v Speaker 1>And the foundation of this rescue mission is the principle of tawhid.

406
00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:12.590
<v Speaker 2>Yes.

407
00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:18.670
<v Speaker 1>We usually translate tawhid as oneness, treating it as a static noun, like a fact you just memorized.

408
00:22:18.670 --> 00:22:19.550
<v Speaker 2>Like a piece of trivia.

409
00:22:19.550 --> 00:22:22.030
<v Speaker 1>Yeah, but it is a verbal noun.

410
00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:23.150
<v Speaker 1>It is an action.

411
00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:27.630
<v Speaker 1>It means affirming the one, yes, but it fundamentally means making one.

412
00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:30.030
<v Speaker 1>It is the active integration of the self.

413
00:22:30.270 --> 00:22:33.710
<v Speaker 2>A scattered, fragmented soul cannot mirror the one.

414
00:22:34.430 --> 00:22:43.470
<v Speaker 2>If your desire for comfort is pulling you one way, your fear of other people's opinions is pulling you another way, and your ambition is pulling you a third way, your soul is divided.

415
00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:44.590
<v Speaker 1>At war with itself.

416
00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:52.830
<v Speaker 2>Tohed is the painful, necessary work of bringing all of those scattered desires under a single, unified orientation toward the real.

417
00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:57.390
<v Speaker 1>Ali summarizes this integration in four staggering sayings of freedom.

418
00:22:57.550 --> 00:22:59.470
<v Speaker 1>He who knows God integrates himself.

419
00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:02.750
<v Speaker 1>He who knows his own soul disengages himself from its lower whims.

420
00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:06.030
<v Speaker 1>He who knows people isolates himself from their drifting currents.

421
00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:09.230
<v Speaker 1>He who knows the world withholds himself from its traps.

422
00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.430
<v Speaker 2>That level of integration requires pure sincerity.

423
00:23:12.990 --> 00:23:19.310
<v Speaker 2>Every single action must be oriented perfectly toward the divine, unadulterated by any foreign motive.

424
00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.350
<v Speaker 1>Especially the motive of making yourself look good.

425
00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:23.310
<v Speaker 2>Exactly.

426
00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:28.750
<v Speaker 2>A Sufi master captured this, tying it directly back to our analogy of the breath and the sounding clay.

427
00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:31.870
<v Speaker 2>He taught that human deeds are but lifeless forms.

428
00:23:32.110 --> 00:23:36.030
<v Speaker 2>Their living spirit is the presence of the secret of sincerity breathed into them.

429
00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:41.470
<v Speaker 1>A deed performed without sincerity, even if it looks like a good deed on the outside, is just sounding clay.

430
00:23:41.470 --> 00:23:43.790
<v Speaker 2>It is structurally perfect, but it is dead.

431
00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:47.870
<v Speaker 1>Sincerity is the in-breathing that lifts a mechanical action into life.

432
00:23:48.430 --> 00:23:53.550
<v Speaker 1>The person who achieves this victory over their own ego attains the rank of the true martyr.

433
00:23:53.950 --> 00:23:58.150
<v Speaker 1>They bear witness to reality by sacrificing their own false centrality.

434
00:23:58.150 --> 00:24:00.830
<v Speaker 2>Which brings us to the ultimate stakes of all this exertion.

435
00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:16.270
<v Speaker 2>It is easy to look at this vast battlefield, the paradoxes that strip our words, the emptying of the mind, the stark council among the graves, the army storming the heart, the perpetual war against Caprice, and conclude that this is a grim, exhausting process of endless subtraction.

436
00:24:16.430 --> 00:24:17.950
<v Speaker 1>It sounds like a lot to lose.

437
00:24:18.390 --> 00:24:23.230
<v Speaker 2>That feeling of grim subtraction is just the illusion of the ego panicking.

438
00:24:23.510 --> 00:24:26.150
<v Speaker 2>What is actually being taken away on this battlefield?

439
00:24:26.190 --> 00:24:27.390
<v Speaker 1>Only the false self.

440
00:24:27.390 --> 00:24:28.510
<v Speaker 2>Only the rust on the heart.

441
00:24:28.510 --> 00:24:30.590
<v Speaker 2>Only the idol of caprice that kept you blind.

442
00:24:31.310 --> 00:24:36.990
<v Speaker 1>When you strip away the ego that hijacked the soul, what is uncovered is not a barren wasteland.

443
00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:44.790
<v Speaker 1>What is uncovered is the pure capacity to see reality exactly as it is and the freedom to live in total harmony with it.

444
00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.190
<v Speaker 2>And that clear sight.

445
00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:48.430
<v Speaker 2>is the beginning of joy.

446
00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:59.870
<v Speaker 2>The one who has rescued their soul, who has integrated it under the intellect, arrives at a fullness and a blinding beauty that makes all the fleeting, flickering pleasures of the world look like faint shadows.

447
00:25:00.430 --> 00:25:04.430
<v Speaker 2>The war, with all its severity, was only ever fought to make room for this joy.

448
00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:08.030
<v Speaker 1>Which leaves us exactly where we began with the central spine of the teaching.

449
00:25:08.110 --> 00:25:13.710
<v Speaker 1>The intellect is a living spiritual faculty realized only through the war to win the whole soul over to its side.

450
00:25:13.870 --> 00:25:15.150
<v Speaker 2>We turn this back to you listening.

451
00:25:15.310 --> 00:25:18.350
<v Speaker 1>The battlefield we have been describing is not historical.

452
00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:20.910
<v Speaker 1>It is interior and it is active right now.

453
00:25:21.150 --> 00:25:23.230
<v Speaker 1>Look at the terrain of your own life this past week.

454
00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:29.630
<v Speaker 2>Which caprice, which hidden egoistic whim have you allowed to take command of the center of your fields?

455
00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:30.270
<v Speaker 1>And what would it

456
00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:35.950
<v Speaker 1>actually cost you to wage the war, to win your soul back, not to destroy it, but to finally rescue it.
